  "id": "Compound::DB00193 - Gene::1576",
  "verbalization": "Tramadol is linked to Cyp3a4 through a series of mechanistic pathways that involve the enzyme cytochrome P450 3A4. The evidence suggests that Tramadol may influence Cyp3a4 through its interaction with other agents that bind to this enzyme. Specifically, Tramadol causes Dizziness Aggravated, which is linked to Tamsulosin, a drug that binds to Cyp3a4. Similarly, Tramadol causes Respiratory Tract Infection Viral, which is linked to Maraviroc, another drug that binds to Cyp3a4. Additionally, Tramadol causes Joint Crepitation, linked to Meloxicam, which also binds to Cyp3a4. These pathways collectively indicate that Tramadol may modulate Cyp3a4 activity through the binding of other agents, potentially affecting drug metabolism. The evidence is supported by the presence of a common ancestor in the chemical entity and the agent affecting the nervous system, suggesting a broader role in metabolic processes. The integration of these pathways highlights the potential for Tramadol to influence Cyp3a4 through a network of interacting agents, though the exact mechanism remains to be fully elucidated.",
